G4521.def:
σάββατον, -ου, τό (Aram. שַׁבָּתָא, transliterated σάββατα, and this being mistaken for a pl., the sing. σάββατον was formed from it), and σάββατα, -ων, τά 
[in LXX for שַׁבָּת, שַׁבָּתוֹן ;] 
__1. the seventh day of the week, the sabbath 
__(a) the sing. form -ον, τὸ σ.: Matthieu 12:8, Marc 2:27, Luc 6:5, al.; ἡ ἡμέρα τοῦ σ. (in LXX, Exode 20:8, al., usually τῶν σ., see infr.), Luc 13:16 14:5; ὁδὸς σαββάτου, Actes 1:12 (cf. Matthieu 24:29); dative, of time (τῷ) σ., Luc 6:9 14:1; ἐν (τῷ) σ., Matthieu 12:2, Luc 6:7, Jean 5:16, al.; accusative, of duration, τὸ σ., Luc 23:56; κατὰ πᾶν σ., Actes 13:27 15:21 18:4; pl., σ. τρία, Actes 17:2 R, txt. (but see infr.); 
__(b) as most frequently in LXX (see Swete, Mk., 17; Thackeray, Gr., 35) the pl. form, τὰ σ. (see supr. on the Aram. form. There is also an analogy in the names of other festivals, τ. ἐγκαίνια, ἄζυμα, etc.): Matthieu 28:1, Colossiens 2:16; ἡ ἡμέρα τῶν σ. (Exode 20:8, al.), Luc 4:16, Actes 13:14 16:13; dative pl. (in LXX -τοις, but 1Ma.2:38 as in NT) by metaplasmus (El., § 9, 3), σάββασι, Matthieu 12:1, 5 12:10-12 Marc 1:21 2:23 3:2, 4, Luc 4:31 6:2. 
__2. seven days, a week; 
__(a) the sing. form: πρώτη σαββάτσυ, Marc 16:9; δὶς τοῦ σ. (Bl., § 35, 4; 36, 13), Luc 18:12; κατὰ μίαν σαββάτου, 1 Corinthiens 16:2; pl., σ. τρία, Actes 17:2 R, mg. (but see supr.); 
__(b) the pl. form: ἡ μία τῶν σ. (where the genitive = μετὰ τά; Soph., Lex., 43a), Matthieu 28:1, Marc 16:2, Luc 24:1, Jean 20:1, 19, Actes 20:7

G4526.def:
σάκκος also written σάκος, -ου, ὁ 
(cf. Heb. שַׂק, which it renders in LXX), 
__1. a coarse cloth, sackcloth, usually made of hair: Apocalypse 6:12. 
__2. Anything made of sackcloth; 
__(a) a sack (Genèse 42:25, al.); 
__(b) a garment of sackcloth, expressive of mourning or penitence: Matthieu 11:21, Luc 10:13, Apocalypse 11:3.†

G4531.def:
σαλεύω
(σάλος), [in LXX for מוֹט, זוּעַ, etc. ;] 
prop., of the action of wind, storm, etc., to agitate, shake: of a reed, Matthieu 11:7, Luc 7:24; a house, Luc 6:48, Actes 4:31 16:26; the earth, Hébreux 12:26; the heavenly bodies, Matthieu 24:29, Marc 13:25, Luc 21:26; of a vessel shaken in filling, Luc 6:38. Metaphorical, 
__(a) to shake, i.e. to render insecure: τὰ σαλευόμενα, τὰ μὴ., Hébreux 12:27; 
__(b) with accusative of person(s), to cast down from a sense of security and happiness: Actes 2:25 (LXX); 
__(with) to unsettle or drive away: pass., before ἀπὸ τ. νοός, 2 Thessaloniciens 2:2; 
__(d) to stir up: τ. ὄχλους, Actes 17:13.†

G4540.def:
Σαμαρία (Rec. -άρεια), -ας, ἡ (Heb. שֹׁמְרוֹן; Aram. שָׁמְרַיִן), 
Samaria; 
__(a) the city: Actes 8:5 (and perhaps also Actes 8:9, 14); 
__(b) the region: Luc 17:11, Jean 4:4-5, 7, Actes 1:8 8:1 (and perhaps also Actes 8:9, 14) Actes 9:31 15:3.†

G4549G.def:
Σαούλ, ὁ indecl. 
(in FlJ, Σάουλος; Heb. שָׁאוּל) 
Saul; 
__(a) the King of Israel: Actes 13:21; 
__(b) the Jewish name of the Apostle Paul, used in the indecl. form only in address (cf. Σαῦλος): Actes 9:4, 17 22:7, 13 26:14.†

G4549H.def:
Σαούλ, ὁ indecl. 
(in FlJ, Σάουλος; Heb. שָׁאוּל) 
Saul; 
__(a) the King of Israel: Actes 13:21; 
__(b) the Jewish name of the Apostle Paul, used in the indecl. form only in address (cf. Σαῦλος): Actes 9:4, 17 22:7, 13 26:14.†

G4561.def:
σάρξ, σαρκός, ἡ 
[in LXX chiefly for בָּשָׂר ;] 
flesh; 
__1. as in cl. generally, 
__(a) prop., of the soft substance of the animal body: 1 Corinthiens 15:39, 2 Corinthiens 12:7, Galates 6:13, al.; σ. καὶ αἷμα, 1 Corinthiens 5:50; σ. καὶ ὀστέα, Luc 24:39; pl., of the flesh of many or parts of the flesh of one (cl.), Apocalypse 17:16 19:18; φαγεῖν, Re, ll. with (cf. κατεσθίειν, 4Ki.9:36, al., and βιβρώσκειν, frequently in l.); metaphorically, Jacques 5:3; mystically, φ. (τρώγειν) τὴν σ. τοῦ υἱοῦ τ. ἀνθρώπου, Jean 6:52-56; 
__(b) Of the whole substance of the body, = σῶμα: Actes 2:26 (LXX), 31, 2 Corinthiens 12:7, Galates 4:14, Éphésiens 5:29; μία σ., Marc 10:8; εἰς σ. μίαν (Genèse 2:24), Matthieu 19:5, Marc 10:8, 1 Corinthiens 6:16, Éphésiens 5:31; hence, of the material as opposite to the immaterial part of man (cf. Lft., Notes, 88): opposite to πνεῦμα, 1 Corinthiens 5:5, 2 Corinthiens 7:1, Colossiens 2:5, 1 Pierre 3:18, 4:6; to ψύχη, Actes 2:31, Rec.; of the present life, ἐν (τῇ) σ., Romains 7:5, Galates 2:20, Philippiens 1:22, 24 1 Pierre 4:2; of Christ's life on earth, αἱ ἡμέραι τ. σαρκὸς αὐτοῦ, Hébreux 5:7; of things pertaining to the body, ἐν (τῇ) σ., Galates 6:12, 13 Philippiens 3:3, 4. 
__2. As in Heb. idiom, 
__(a) of a living creature: πᾶσα σ. (Heb. כָּל־בָּשָׂר; cf. Bl., §47, 9), Matthieu 24:22, Marc 13:20, 1 Pierre 1:24; esp. of man and his mortality (Psa.55 (56):5, Sir.28:5, al.), Jean 1:14; πᾶσα σ. (see supr.), Luc 3:6, Jean 17:2, Actes 2:17, ἐν σ., 1Jo.4:2, 1 Timothée 3:16; 
__(b) of natural origin and relationship (Genèse 2:24, Ésaïe 58:7, al.): τέκνα τῆς σ., Romains 9:8; κατὰ σάρκα, ib. 3, 5, 1 Corinthiens 10:18, Galates 4:23, 29; ἡ σ. μου, Romains 11:14 (cf. Juges 9:2, 2 Rois 5:1, al.). 
__3. Of the physical nature as subject to sensation and desire (Plut.), 
__(a) without any ethical disparagement: Romains 7:18 13:14; opposite to πνεῦμα, Matthieu 26:41, Marc 14:38; τ. θέλημα τῆς σ., Jean 1:13; ἡ ἐπιθυμία τῆς σ., 1 Jean 2:16; pl., 2 Pierre 2:18; παθεῖν σαρκί, 1 Pierre 4:1; 
__(b) in ethical sense, esp. in Pauline Epp., of the flesh as the seat and vehicle of sinful desires: opposite to νοῦς, Romains 7:25; to πνεῦμα, Romains 8:4-9, 12, 13, Galates 5:16, 17 19 6:8 (cf. DB, ii, 14f.; iv, 165f.; Cremer, 844ff.).

G4575.def:
σεβαστός, -ή, -όν (σεβάζομαι) 
__1. reverend, august. 
__2. In late writers, with reference to the Roman Imperial name; 
__(a) ὁ Σ., Augustus, i.e. the Roman Emperor: Actes 25:21, 25; 
__(b) Augustan: σπείρα σ., Actes 27:1 (See further, Deiss., BS, 218.) †

G4592.def:
σημεῖον, -ου, τό 
[in LXX chiefly for אוֹת ;] 
a sign, mark, token; 
__(a) of that which distinguishes a person or thing from others: Matthieu 26:48 Luc 2:12, 2 Thessaloniciens 3:17 (cf. Deiss., LAE, 153.2); before genitive epexeg., Romains 4:11; with genitive obj., Matthieu 24:8, 30, 2 Corinthiens 12:12; with genitive subj., Matthieu 16:3; 
__(b) a sign of warning or admonition: Matthieu 12:39 16:4, Luc 2:34 11:29-30, 1 Corinthiens 14:22; 
__(with) a sign portending future events (Soph., Plat. al.): Marc 13:4, Luc 21:7, 11 21:25, Actes 2:19, Apocalypse 12:1, 3 15:1; 
__(d) of miracles and wonders (MM, xxii), regarded as signs of a divine authority: Matthieu 12:38-39 16:1, 4 Marc 8:11-12 Luc 11:16, 29 23:8, Jean 2:11, 18 2:23 4:54 6:30 10:41 12:18, Actes 4:16, 22; pl., Marc 16:17, 20, Jean 2:11, 23 3:2 6:2, 14 6:26 7:31 9:16 11:47 12:37 20:30, Actes 8:6, 1 Corinthiens 1:22 (Lft., Notes, 162); the same ascribed to false teachers and demons: Matthieu 24:24, Marc 13:22, 2 Thessaloniciens 2:9, Apocalypse 13:13-14 16:14 19:20; σ. καὶ τέρατα (τ. καὶ σ.; cf. Tr., Syn., § xci), Matthieu 24:24, Marc 13:22, Jean 4:48, Actes 2:19, 43 4:30 5:12 6:8 7:36 14:3 15:12, Romains 15:19, 2 Thessaloniciens 2:9; id. before καὶ δυνάμεις, 2 Corinthiens 12:12, Hébreux 2:4; σ. καὶ δυνάμεις, Actes 8:13; δ. καὶ τ. καὶ σ., Actes 2:22; σ. διδόναι, Matthieu 24:24, Marc 13:22.†
